  m_SharedData: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 99a80a90d5fbacd47a07de054c834149, type: 2}
  m_Metadata:
    m_Items: []
  m_TableData:
  - m_Id: 20270153728
    m_Localized: 'In this first simulation we will explore one of the fundamental
      principles of population genetics: the Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. This concept
      describes a theoretical situation in which allele and genotype frequencies
      in a population remain constant from generation to generation.


      In our
      case, we are working with a single locus with two alleles (A and a), both with
      an initial frequency of 0.5. Under random mating, the expected genotype frequencies
      after one generation would be:


      AA (light red) = 0.25

      Aa (medium
      red) = 0.5

      aa (dark red) = 0.25


      Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium is
      only maintained if certain conditions are met:


      - Random mating (this
      simulation fulfills this condition).

      - Absence of evolutionary forces:
      selection, mutation, migration, and genetic drift.


      Among these forces,
      there is one that never completely disappears in real populations: genetic
      drift.


'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 2760845164544
    m_Localized: 'Genetic drift is the random change in allele frequencies due to
      chance. It does not depend on whether an allele is advantageous or disadvantageous,
      it occurs simply because of stochastic processes.


      There are two main
      sources of this drift:


      - Gametic sampling: each offspring receives
      one of the two alleles from each parent at a given locus, and this transmission
      is random. For example, an Aa individual (medium red) has a 50% probability
      of passing on allele A and a 50% probability of passing on allele a.


      -
      Variation in reproductive success: not all individuals leave exactly the same
      number of offspring. Even if all individuals have the same probability of reproducing,
      the final number of offspring can vary simply by chance.


      These two
      sources of randomness have a much stronger effect when the population is small.
      Imagine a population consisting of only two individuals, both Aa. If they produce
      two offspring and, by chance, both offspring inherit only allele A from their
      parents, in a single generation the allele frequencies would shift from 0.5
      / 0.5 to 1 / 0. That population would have completely lost allele a purely
      by chance.


      In large populations, by contrast, individual fluctuations
      tend to balance each other out. Although randomness is still acting, its relative
      effect is much smaller. In the theoretical case of an infinite population,
      genetic drift would disappear.


      In this simulation, we explicitly model
      each individual and each reproductive event. This means that in both populations,
      chance operates in every generation. However, the population on the left is
      much larger than the one on the right, so fluctuations in allele and genotype
      frequencies will be smaller and more stable over time. All the changes observed
      in these frequencies are due solely to population size, since the other conditions
      required for equilibrium are fulfilled.'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1289088297435136
    m_Localized: 'In this second simulation we start from the same initial conditions:
      two populations, one large and one small, both with initial allele frequencies
      of 0.5 for A and 0.5 for a. As in the previous case, random mating occurs and
      there is no selection or mutation.


      The key difference now is that migration
      is introduced. In each generation, individuals from the large population migrate
      into the small population.


      Migration is another evolutionary force.
      It involves the movement of individuals between populations and their subsequent
      reproduction there. When migrants reproduce in their new population, they introduce
      their alleles into that gene pool. This process is also known as gene flow.


      In
      this simulation, the large population acts as a reservoir of genetic diversity.
      Because it is large, genetic drift has only a weak effect on it, and its allele
      frequencies tend to remain close to the initial 0.5 / 0.5 ratio. When migrants
      move into the small population, they continuously reintroduce both alleles.


      In
      the absence of migration, as you saw in the previous example, the small population
      would be strongly affected by genetic drift, and one allele could easily become
      fixed (frequency = 1) while the other is lost (frequency = 0). However, with
      ongoing migration, lost alleles can be reintroduced and extreme fluctuations
      are reduced.


      As a result, gene flow counteracts the effects of genetic
      drift in the small population. The small population maintains higher genetic
      diversity and tends to remain closer to Hardy-Weinberg expectations over time,
      although drift is never completely eliminated.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1294533296750592
    m_Localized: "En la esquina inferior derecha de la ventana encontrar\xE1s un
      bot\xF3n que permite descargar o copiar al portapapeles los datos de la poblaci\xF3n
      en ese instante en formato Genepop. Puedes utilizar ese archivo para acceder
      a Genepop On The Web y analizar la poblaci\xF3n all\xED, aplicando tests estad\xEDsticos
      para evaluar posibles desviaciones del equilibrio de Hardy-Weinberg. Esto te
      permitir\xE1 cuantificar c\xF3mo de lejos est\xE1 cada poblaci\xF3n del equilibrio
      en distintos momentos de la simulaci\xF3n. Esta opci\xF3n est\xE1 disponible
      en todas las simulaciones de la aplicaci\xF3n.\n\nUPDATE: No se desde hace
      cuanto pero Genepop On The Web est\xE1 caida :("
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1294651064418304
    m_Localized: "En esta simulaci\xF3n, usando los datos en formato Genepop, compara
      qu\xE9 tan cerca del equilibrio de Hardy-Weinberg se encuentra ahora la poblaci\xF3n
      peque\xF1a (la de la derecha) en comparaci\xF3n con la simulaci\xF3n anterior.
      Observa si la migraci\xF3n reduce las desviaciones causadas por la deriva gen\xE9tica
      y c\xF3mo influye en el mantenimiento de la diversidad al\xE9lica a lo largo
      del tiempo.\n\nUPDATE: No se desde hace cuanto pero Genepop On The Web est\xE1
      caida :("
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1617382470918144
    m_Localized: 'In this simulation we move beyond simple discrete alleles and explore
      a quantitative trait influenced by mutation and selection. Instead of having
      just two alleles, this locus can take a range of values between 0 and 255.
      Each value corresponds to a shade of blue:

      0 represents RGB (0,0,0),
      while 255 represents RGB (0,0,255). In total, there are 255 possible allelic
      values.


      The phenotype is determined by the average of the two allelic
      values carried by an individual. This corresponds to incomplete dominance,
      where heterozygotes show an intermediate phenotype between the two homozygotes.


      We
      have two large populations. In both cases, mutation occurs. However, only one
      population is subject to natural selection. At the start of the simulation,
      all individuals in both populations are homozygous for allele value 128. This
      means there is initially no variation: every individual has the same genotype
      and the same phenotype.


      Mutations can either increase or decrease the
      allelic value. Over time, this generates new genetic variation in both populations.


      In
      the population on the left, color has no effect on survival or reproduction.
      It is a neutral trait, and changes in allele values are driven only by mutation
      and genetic drift, both of which are random and non-directional.


      In
      contrast, in the population on the right, there is directional selection favoring
      higher allele values. That is, individuals with a more intense blue coloration
      have higher reproductive success. As a result, higher allele values tend to
      become more frequent over generations.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1628786993209344
    m_Localized: "In this simulation we explore a fundamental question in evolutionary
      biology:\r\nIs Mendelian (particulate) inheritance essential for natural selection
      to produce long-term evolutionary change? \r\n\r\nWe begin with two large populations
      under the same selective pressure. In both cases, lighter green individuals
      have higher reproductive success. The initial allele frequencies are 0.2 for
      light green and 0.8 for dark green.\r\n\r\nHowever, the two populations differ
      in one crucial aspect: the mechanism of inheritance.\r\n\r\nIn the population
      on the left, inheritance follows the historical model known as blending inheritance.
      This was the commonly accepted view of heredity in Darwin\u2019s time. Under
      this model, offspring phenotypes are simple intermediate mixtures of their
      parents.\r\n\r\nIt is important not to confuse blending inheritance with incomplete
      dominance. In incomplete dominance (which can occur under Mendelian genetics),
      alleles remain intact and discrete, even though the heterozygote shows an intermediate
      phenotype. The key point is that the underlying genetic variants are preserved
      and can reappear unchanged in future generations.\r\n\r\nIn blending inheritance,
      by contrast, the genetic variants themselves are averaged in each generation.
      You can think of individuals as effectively carrying a single trait value per
      locus, which is the result of mixing (in this simulation, the average) of the
      parental values. Once mixed, the original variants cannot be recovered. As
      a result over generations, differences between individuals tend to disappear.\r\n\r\nIn
      the population on the right, inheritance is Mendelian, or particulate. Alleles
      are transmitted intact from parents to offspring. They do not blend or disappear
      when combined in heterozygotes. Each generation reshuffles existing alleles
      through segregation, but the underlying genetic variants remain discrete. In
      this population, phenotypes are produced through incomplete dominance.\r\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 1628917138268160
    m_Localized: "Under blending inheritance, variation is progressively diluted
      every generation. Even if selection favors lighter individuals, as variation
      decreases, the raw material on which selection acts disappears.\r\n\r\nUnder
      Mendelian inheritance, by contrast, variation is preserved. Even if individuals
      with intermediate phenotypes are common, the underlying alleles remain intact
      and can be passed on unchanged. This allows advantageous alleles to increase
      steadily in frequency over generations. \r\n\r\nHistorically, Charles Darwin
      proposed natural selection without knowing the mechanism of inheritance. If
      inheritance had truly been blending, as he thought, natural selection could
      not have been a driver of adaptation. The later integration of Mendelian genetics
      with evolutionary theory showed that particulate inheritance provides exactly
      what selection requires: stable, heritable variation.\r\n\r\nBy comparing these
      two populations, you can observe how the same selective pressure produces very
      different evolutionary outcomes depending solely on the mechanism of inheritance.\r\n"
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3496160503603200
    m_Localized: 'We have already seen the effects of genetic drift, mutation, selection,
      and, to a lesser extent, migration. These evolutionary processes can produce
      important changes in populations. However, evolutionary change is not always
      beneficial: it can reduce genetic diversity or even favor the fixation of alleles
      that are not adaptive.



      As we have already seen, this can occur through
      genetic drift. Especially in small populations, stochastic processes can lead
      to the fixation of alleles and the loss of genetic diversity, regardless of
      whether those alleles are beneficial or harmful to individuals.



      Another
      process that can also promote the fixation of potentially non-adaptive alleles
      is gene flow. In this simulation we will examine a first case involving two
      populations: a very large population in which the black allele is fixed, and
      a much smaller population in which the blue allele is fixed.



      When
      these two populations come into contact, gene flow begins to occur from the
      population on the left, which acts as the **source population**, toward the
      population on the right, which functions as the **sink population**.



      As
      individuals from the source population migrate into the smaller population,
      they begin to introduce the black allele. In the absence of natural selection
      favoring the blue allele in the small population, this gene flow progressively
      modifies allele frequencies. Over time, the black allele becomes increasingly
      common in the small population, while the blue allele begins to decline. Eventually,
      the blue allele disappears completely, being replaced by the black allele coming
      from the source population.



      This first simulation illustrates how
      gene flow can eliminate genetic differences between populations. When migration
      occurs continuously and there is no selection maintaining those differences,
      populations tend to become genetically similar.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3496316187779072
    m_Localized: 'For this second simulation imagine that these two populations were
      once separated for a long period of time. During that time, the population
      on the right adapted to different environmental conditions, which favored the
      blue phenotype. As a result, the blue allele became fixed in that population.



      Now,
      the two populations come back into contact and migration begins to occur again
      from the large population on the left (the source population), where the black
      allele is fixed, toward the small population on the right (the sink population).



      However,
      unlike the previous simulation, the blue phenotype now has a selective advantage
      in the small population. Individuals with a more intense blue coloration have
      higher reproductive success in this environment.



      This creates two
      opposing evolutionary forces. Migration continuously introduces the black allele
      into the small population, while natural selection favors the blue allele already
      present there.



      As a result, neither allele completely replaces the
      other. Instead, the population reaches an equilibrium where both forces balance
      each other. The phenotype distribution stabilizes at an intermediate point
      between black and blue.



      This situation illustrates an important principle
      in population genetics: when evolutionary forces act in opposite directions,
      populations can reach stable equilibria where genetic diversity is maintained
      over time.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3499390650359808
    m_Localized: 'In this third simulation we start from the same situation as in
      the previous one: the population on the right is adapted to its environment
      and the blue phenotype has a selective advantage. However, the migration rate
      from the source population is now much higher.



      This means that in
      each generation many more individuals carrying the black allele arrive from
      the large population on the left into the small population on the right.



      Although
      selection still favors the blue phenotype in the small population, the force
      of gene flow is now much stronger than that of natural selection.



      As
      a result, the black allele progressively increases in frequency until it eventually
      becomes fixed in the small population as well, even though the blue phenotype
      was the most favorable in that environment.



      This final simulation
      illustrates an important point: evolution is not always adaptive, meaning that
      it does not always lead to the optimal adaptation to the environment. In this
      case, gene flow is strong enough to counteract local selection and ultimately
      impose an allele that is not the most adaptive for that population.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  - m_Id: 3502944333144064
    m_Localized: 'In this second simulation we again compare two populations that
      differ only in their mechanism of inheritance: blending (non-Mendelian) on
      the left and Mendelian (particulate) on the right.

      

      Unlike
      the previous simulation, we now introduce a constant source of favorable alleles.
      In every generation, individuals carrying the green allele are continuously
      added to both populations. This provides the raw material for evolution: both
      populations have the potential to evolve toward a fully green phenotype.

      

      However,
      the way inheritance works makes a dramatic difference. In the population on
      the right, with Mendelian inheritance, alleles remain discrete and are transmitted
      intact from parents to offspring. This allows the population to respond rapidly
      to the input of favorable alleles, and the phenotype quickly shifts toward
      full green.

      

      In the population on the left, with blending inheritance,
      the effect of new favorable alleles is diluted each generation. Because traits
      are averaged in the offspring, it takes much longer for the population to accumulate
      the green phenotype. Evolution toward full green occurs, but at a drastically
      slower pace.

'
    m_Metadata:
      m_Items: []
  references:
    version: 2
    RefIds: []
